 ## Overview
-Composite Image Loss with Visibility Weighting is a training objective for differentiable volume rendering that combines photometric fidelity terms (L1, SSIM, LPIPS) with auxiliary regularization losses, where the photometric terms are modulated by a visibility mask to suppress errors in background or occluded regions [S5]. It sets itself apart from standard photometric losses by explicitly incorporating a visibility map $V_M$ to weight the pixel-wise reconstruction error, ensuring the network focuses supervision only on visible foreground content, while also allowing for the inclusion of silhouette consistency via binary cross-entropy [S4, S5].
+Composite Image Loss with Visibility Weighting is a training objective for differentiable volume rendering that combines photometric fidelity terms (L1, SSIM, LPIPS) with auxiliary regularization losses, where the photometric terms are modulated by a visibility mask to suppress errors in background or occluded regions [S6]. It sets itself apart from standard photometric losses by explicitly incorporating a visibility map $V_M$ to weight the pixel-wise reconstruction error, ensuring the network focuses supervision only on visible foreground content, while also allowing for the inclusion of silhouette consistency via binary cross-entropy [S4, S6].
 
